{"files": [{"id": 47099653, "name": "ppat.1011970.s006.xlsx", "size": 110578, "is_link_only": false, "download_url": "https://ndownloader.figshare.com/files/47099653", "supplied_md5": "e66fcdc8e17ff58599ae49d41eaa184e", "computed_md5": "e66fcdc8e17ff58599ae49d41eaa184e", "mimetype": "application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.spreadsheetml.sheet"}], "folder_structure": {}, "authors": [{"id": 4308, "full_name": "Yan Chen", "first_name": "Yan", "last_name": "Chen", "is_active": false, "url_name": "Yan_Chen", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 18841048, "full_name": "Rodney P. Kincaid", "first_name": "Rodney P.", "last_name": "Kincaid", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 18841051, "full_name": "Kelley Bastin", "first_name": "Kelley", "last_name": "Bastin", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 18841054, "full_name": "Devin N. Fachko", "first_name": "Devin N.", "last_name": "Fachko", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}, {"id": 18841057, "full_name": "Rebecca L. Skalsky", "first_name": "Rebecca L.", "last_name": "Skalsky", "is_active": false, "url_name": "_", "orcid_id": ""}], "custom_fields": [], "figshare_url": "https://plos.figshare.com/articles/dataset/PAR-CLIP_Sites_with_Matches_to_miR-142_/26051920", "download_disabled": false, "description": "<div><p>Reactivation from latency plays a significant role in maintaining persistent lifelong Epstein-Barr virus (EBV) infection. Mechanisms governing successful activation and progression of the EBV lytic phase are not fully understood. EBV expresses multiple viral microRNAs (miRNAs) and manipulates several cellular miRNAs to support viral infection. To gain insight into the host miRNAs regulating transitions from EBV latency into the lytic stage, we conducted a CRISPR/Cas9-based screen in EBV+ Burkitt lymphoma (BL) cells using anti-Ig antibodies to crosslink the B cell receptor (BCR) and induce reactivation. Using a gRNA library against >1500 annotated human miRNAs, we identified miR-142 as a key regulator of EBV reactivation. Genetic ablation of miR-142 enhanced levels of immediate early and early lytic gene products in infected BL cells. Ago2-PAR-CLIP experiments with reactivated cells revealed miR-142 targets related to Erk/MAPK signaling, including components directly downstream of the B cell receptor (BCR). Consistent with these findings, disruption of miR-142 enhanced SOS1 levels and Mek phosphorylation in response to surface Ig cross-linking. Effects could be rescued by inhibitors of Mek (cobimetinib) or Raf (dabrafenib). Taken together, these results show that miR-142 functionally regulates SOS1/Ras/Raf/Mek/Erk signaling initiated through the BCR and consequently, restricts EBV entry into the lytic cycle.</p></div>", "funding": null, "funding_list": [], "version": 1, "status": "public", "size": 110578, "created_date": "2024-06-17T17:37:24Z", "modified_date": "2024-06-28T17:35:35Z", "is_public": true, "is_confidential": false, "is_metadata_record": false, "confidential_reason": "", "metadata_reason": "", "license": {"value": 1, "name": "CC BY 4.0", "url": "https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/"}, "tags": ["b cell receptor", "surface ig cross", "xlink \"> reactivation", "142 targets related", "142 enhanced levels", "restricts ebv entry", "support viral infection", "infected bl cells", "cells using anti", "ebv lytic phase", "erk signaling initiated", "ebv reactivation", "mapk signaling", "lytic stage", "ig antibodies", "induce reactivation", "ebv latency", "taken together", "significant role", "results show", "latency plays", "key regulator", "immediate early", "grna library", "genetic ablation", "gain insight", "fully understood", "effects could", "dabrafenib ).", "clip experiments", "burkitt lymphoma", "based screen", "barr virus"], "keywords": ["b cell receptor", "surface ig cross", "xlink \"> reactivation", "142 targets related", "142 enhanced levels", "restricts ebv entry", "support viral infection", "infected 